  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on calculating an aspiration level (mean of outcomes = 2333.33) and comparing each lottery based on the probability that their outcomes exceed this level. This requires evaluating whether 4000 and 3000 are above the aspiration (they are) and then using their respective probabilities (20% and 25%) as the probabilities of positive deviations. The individual does consider the probabilities of winning (i.e., getting a non-zero outcome) and notes that Lottery B has a higher chance despite a lower amount. While this aligns with using the probability of a positive outcome as a criterion, there is no mention or indication of computing an aspiration level or evaluating outcomes relative to such a benchmark. The reasoning appears more aligned with a simple 'maximize chance of winning' heuristic rather than a structured comparison relative to an aspiration level derived from all outcomes. The similarity lies in the use of probabilities of non-zero (and above-aspiration) outcomes, but the conceptual foundation (aspiration level as a mean of all outcomes) is absent in the verbal report. Therefore, while the final comparison involves similar numerical values, the reasoning process described does not reflect the construction or use of the aspiration level as defined.",
  "final_assessment_score": 30
}

